[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Threads and Processes in Data Structures & Algorithms"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding threads and processes is crucial for designing efficient and scalable algorithms and data structures. They are fundamental concepts in operating systems and concurrent programming, impacting performance and resource utilization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Processes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A process is an independent execution environment with its own memory space, resources, and program counter. It's a heavyweight entity, meaning creating, managing, and switching between processes is relatively expensive."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Each process has its own address space, preventing direct access to the memory of other processes (unless using inter-process communication)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Processes are isolated, meaning a crash in one process typically doesn't affect other processes."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating a new process involves duplicating the parent process's memory space (or using copy-on-write), which is resource-intensive."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Threads"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process. Multiple threads can exist within a single process, sharing the same memory space and resources. This allows for concurrent execution of different parts of a program."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Threads share the same address space, allowing them to easily access and modify the same data. This can lead to race conditions and data corruption if not properly synchronized."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating and switching between threads is generally faster and less resource-intensive than processes."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If one thread crashes, it can potentially bring down the entire process, affecting all other threads within that process."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Summarized"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Processes: Independent, heavyweight, isolated memory spaces."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Threads: Dependent, lightweight, shared memory space."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Concurrency vs. Parallelism"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's important to distinguish between concurrency and parallelism."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concurrency: Deals with managing multiple tasks at the same time. It's about structuring a program to handle multiple tasks, even if they don't all run simultaneously. Threads enable concurrency."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parallelism: Deals with actually executing multiple tasks simultaneously, typically on multiple CPU cores. Processes and threads can be used to achieve parallelism."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Inter-Process Communication (IPC)"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Since processes have separate memory spaces, they need mechanisms to communicate with each other. Common IPC methods include:"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pipes: For one-way communication between related processes (e.g., parent and child)."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Message Queues: Allow processes to exchange messages."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shared Memory: Allows processes to access a common region of memory (requires careful synchronization)."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sockets: For communication between processes on the same or different machines."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Thread Synchronization"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because threads share memory, synchronization mechanisms are essential to prevent race conditions and ensure data integrity. Common techniques include:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mutexes (Mutual Exclusion Locks): Allow only one thread to access a critical section of code at a time."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Semaphores: Control access to a shared resource by maintaining a counter."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Condition Variables: Allow threads to wait for a specific condition to become true."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Scenario: Parallel Sorting"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a large array that needs to be sorted. We can divide the array into smaller chunks and assign each chunk to a separate thread for sorting. After all threads have finished sorting their chunks, the chunks can be merged to produce the final sorted array. This is an example of parallel processing using threads."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"// Example (Conceptual - language agnostic)
function parallelSort(array, numThreads) {
  let chunkSize = array.length / numThreads;
  let threads = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
    let start = i * chunkSize;
    let end = (i === numThreads - 1) ? array.length : (i + 1) * chunkSize;
    let chunk = array.slice(start, end);

    // Create a thread to sort the chunk (implementation details omitted)
    let thread = createThread(sortChunk, chunk);
    threads.push(thread);
    thread.start();
  }

  // Wait for all threads to finish
  waitForAllThreads(threads);

  // Merge the sorted chunks (implementation details omitted)
  let sortedArray = mergeChunks(threads.map(t => t.result));
  return sortedArray;
}"},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing Between Threads and Processes"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between threads and processes depends on the specific application requirements:"},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use threads when: Sharing data is necessary, performance is critical, and the risk of a single crash affecting the entire application is acceptable."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use processes when: Isolation is important, security is a concern, and the application can tolerate the overhead of inter-process communication."},{"order":38,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"Threads and processes are fundamental concepts for concurrent and parallel programming. Understanding their differences and trade-offs is essential for designing efficient and robust data structures and algorithms."}]
